    "failure_scenario": "`_async_occupied` builds addresses index-major with types sorted, so 'password' sorts before 'pin' and the password read runs first. If it describes any slot, `reports[lock]=True` and `on_reports()` fires. The PIN read then runs `silent -= cannot_tell`, so PIN slots the lock cannot report become readings of a held code. Allocation over-reserves them and the lock is no longer listed by `locks_that_cannot_report`. Being able to report one type says nothing about reporting PINs."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/ha/adapter.py",
    "line": 631,
    "summary": "Suppression of the provider echoing its own write was narrowed from the whole slot to one credential address, so pushes about the slot's other types during a write or release now reach the worker as if the lock had reported them.",
    "failure_scenario": "During a password write, or a release that clears every type, a provider pushes the slot's PIN through `push_update` (which always maps to the PIN address). `_writing` is the password address, so `address == self._writing` is False. The provider's own mid-write or cached PIN state is pushed into the worker as a lock report. Before this PR, `_writing` was the slot number, which covered every push for that slot during the write."
  },
