    "failure_scenario": "On a lock that takes PIN and password, the PIN read times out (NO_ANSWER) and the password read answers. `failed` has 1 entry and `by_type` has 2, so nothing is raised. The worker gets a non-empty readings dict, counts it as an answer, and stores every PIN address as SILENT. Backoff, the `lock_offline` repair and the no-answer counting that used to follow a failed PIN read never happen. The same applies to SLOT_REFUSED: a past-capacity PIN probe now ends an outage's backoff if the password read answers, which is the #1398 guard this code exists to keep."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/ha/allocation.py",
    "line": 562,
    "summary": "`_async_occupied` now reads both types through the read that drops a failed type, so if one type's read fails, slots holding that type count as free when they should count as unknown.",
    "failure_scenario": "Slot 5 holds only a password and the password read fails while the PIN read answers EMPTY. The readings for slot 5 contain only PIN EMPTY, so `_async_occupied` reports it free. Before this change, a failed read raised and occupancy became None. The slot is then handed to a new user, LCM writes the PIN and, with the password want at None, clears the existing password. The docstring itself says the cost of under-reserving is 'the code on their door'."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/ha/runtime.py",
    "line": 655,
    "summary": "Which non-PIN types are tracked is decided once, from `managed_credential_types` when the lock is held. When capabilities are cached later and narrow that set, nothing re-evaluates it until some unrelated want change calls `async_want`.",
    "failure_scenario": "A provider that writes passwords is held before `cached_capabilities` is set, so the set is MANAGED & writable = {PIN, PASSWORD}. The lock turns out not to advertise passwords. A password address is still tracked for every slot, and the worker keeps reading and clearing a type the lock does not take, possibly holding the slot and raising `slot_suspended`. binary_sensor.py:65 takes the same snapshot, so a `password_in_sync` entity is created and stays unavailable."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/ha/runtime.py",
    "line": 913,
    "summary": "The `slot_disabled` repair is now deleted only when every credential type in the slot is in sync. A held or out-of-sync password keeps the repair open after the PIN is re-enabled and on the lock.",
    "failure_scenario": "A PIN is refused and the slot is turned off (`slot_disabled`). The user fixes the PIN and re-enables the slot, and the PIN reaches IN_SYNC. Meanwhile the slot's password clear is held (CANT_CLEAR) or still out of sync, so `in_sync` is False and `slot_disabled_{entry}_{slot}` is never deleted. Before this change, a PIN that was wanted and in sync was enough to clear it."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/ha/adapter.py",
    "line": 456,
    "summary": "On a release, the password key still sends its own plain clear, even though the PIN's release already removes every type. Each released slot costs an extra provider exchange plus a read-back, and the outcome depends on which key runs first.",
    "failure_scenario": "`give_up` marks both keys release=True. If the password job runs first, it calls `async_clear_slot_credential(slot, PASSWORD)` as a plain clear. On a native-user provider that can delete the tagged user's last credential, so the PIN release that follows finds nothing with `written=True`. If the PIN release runs first, the password clear is a wasted round trip that returns False and then triggers a read-back."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/_base.py",
    "line": 1539,
    "summary": "Each read exchange now calls `async_get_users` once per credential type (once for the PIN projection and again for the password projection), where one call could serve both types.",
    "failure_scenario": "On a projection-based provider (virtual now; Matter or Z-Wave User Credential once they write passwords), every poll, read-back and allocation read fetches the full user list twice. For devices where `async_get_users` walks the lock over the radio, that doubles read time and mesh traffic. One `async_get_users` call, projected per type, would do the same job."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/ha/runtime.py",
    "line": 656,
    "summary": "Password wants are registered one slot at a time after `hold()`, so each queues its own sync job. The PIN wants are batched into the worker's first read; these are not.",
    "failure_scenario": "On an entry with 30 slots, holding a virtual lock does one batched PIN read and then 30 separate password jobs. Each one is a read exchange, and each read calls `async_get_users` over every slot, so startup work grows roughly quadratically. Including the non-PIN addresses in the `hold(...)` call would let the first read cover them all."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/entity.py",
    "line": 433,
    "summary": "`_addresses` scans every address the worker tracks on each property access. It runs several times per state write (`available`, `is_on`, `extra_state_attributes` via `_core_status` and `_core_syncing`) for every slot entity, which replaces what used to be a single dict lookup.",
    "failure_scenario": "On each lock-changed signal, N slots × several entities × several properties each iterate all of `worker.slots`, which is O(N²) per update instead of O(N). Building `CredentialAddress(slot, t)` for the viewed types and looking each up in `worker.slots` avoids the scan."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/ha/adapter.py",
    "line": 453,
    "summary": "After a release, the lag timestamps are set for `writable_credential_types`, but the release itself clears `managed_credential_types` (base `async_release_managed_slot`), so the two sets can differ.",
